{{also|de-toxify}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|de-toxify}}

===Etymology===
Attested since 1905 (twelve years earlier than {{m|en|toxify}}), from {{affix|en|de-|tox|alt2=tox-|-ify}}. ({{m|en|detoxicate|Detoxicate}} is attested one year earlier, since 1904.)

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[remove]] [[foreign]] and [[harmful]] [[substance]]s from something.
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Transactions of the American Pediatric Society|volume=18|page=295|year=1907|passage=As a result of this, in some way or other, unknown to us, a diminished power of oxidation results and the organism loses the power to '''detoxify''' substances absorbed from the intestine which have been present there in excess; {{...|these circulate in the blood, exerting their poisonous action and cannot be excreted by the kidneys because they are not brought to them in the proper form.}}|title=Some possible etiological factors in the recurrent vomiting of children|author=John Howland}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=http://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/may/05/front-national-family-feud-goes-nuclear-as-jean-marie-le-pen-disowns-marine|title=Front National family feud goes nuclear as Jean-Marie Le Pen disowns Marine|date=2015-05-05|author=Angelique Chrisafis|work=The Guardian|passage=The severe disciplinary action taken by his daughter—who since taking over the FN in 2011 has led a drive to “'''detoxify'''” the party and move it away from the racist, jackbooted, antisemitic imagery of the past—was supposed to silence her father. }}
# To make something that is [[harmful]] more [[benign]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2023| title=The Two Moralities: Conservatives, Liberals, and the Roots of Our Political Divide|author=Ronnie Janoff-Bulman | page=2| passage=We can either break as a nation or find a way to '''detoxify''' our politics.}}

